	abstract = {Optical lattices have developed into a widely used and highly recognized tool to study many-body quantum physics with special relevance for solid state type systems. One of the most prominent reasons for this success is the high degree of tunability in the experimental setups. While at the beginning quasi-static, cubic geometries were mainly explored, the focus of the ﬁeld has now shifted toward new lattice topologies and the dynamical control of lattice structures. In this review we intend to give an overview of the progress recently achieved in this ﬁeld on the experimental side. In addition, we discuss theoretical proposals exploiting speciﬁcally these novel lattice geometries. This article was invited by Professor Gordon Baym.},
